# Oak space — org {{org}}

This directory is an **Oak space** for the `{{org}}` org. It is not a
checkout. A space spans the **whole org**, not a single repo: each task
gets its own subdirectory, and inside it you `oak mount` whichever of
`{{org}}`'s repos the task touches — a lazy, FUSE-backed view per repo,
each on its own virtual branch. **One subdirectory = one concurrent
task** (which may span several repos). Spaces are like git worktrees,
but without a full clone behind each one: mounts hydrate file content on
demand, so spinning up a fresh task is near-instant and cheap.

Because a space covers the org, cross-repo changes live side by side and
you can pull in any repo for extra context when a task needs it.

This file ships with `oak space new {{org}}` and is meant to be read by
the coding agent (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, etc.) opened here.

## Starting a new task

When the user asks you to start work on a new task:

1. **Pick a slug.** 2–4 word kebab-case identifier
   (e.g. `fix-auth-redirect`, `add-search-index`). Confirm with the
   user if ambiguous.

2. **Decide which repo(s) the task needs.** List the org's repos:
   ```
   oak space repos
   ```
   This calls the API and prints every repo in `{{org}}` you can see
   (e.g. `{{org}}/web`, `{{org}}/api`). Pick the one the change belongs
   in — or several, for a cross-repo change. If it's unclear which repo
   owns the work, ask the user or mount a likely candidate read-only to
   look around.

3. **Start a mount per repo, under the task directory.**
   ```
   oak mount {{org}}/<repo> ./<slug>/<repo>
   ```
   Run this once for each repo the task needs. So a single-repo task on
   `web` becomes `./<slug>/web/`, and a cross-repo task gets
   `./<slug>/web/` and `./<slug>/api/` side by side. Each mount creates a
   new virtual branch named `<repo>--<id8>` off that repo's default
   branch and runs as a detached background daemon — the command returns
   as soon as the mount is live. Mount-aware commands (`oak status`,
   `oak commit`, etc., run from inside a repo's mount) pick up the
   virtual branch automatically.

4. **Read each repo's own AGENTS.md.** Agents walk up from cwd to find
   their instructions file, but cwd stays at the space root — so a
   repo's instructions won't auto-load. Read `./<slug>/<repo>/AGENTS.md`
   explicitly if it exists, for every repo you mounted.

So a space for `{{org}}` might hold:

```
{{org}}/                  # the space (created by `oak space new {{org}}`)
├── AGENTS.md             # this file
├── CLAUDE.md             # one-line `@AGENTS.md` import (for Claude Code)
├── .oak-space            # marker recording the org ({{org}})
├── .claude/
│   └── settings.json     # space settings (Stop hook + permissions)
├── add-search/           # task 1 — touches one repo
│   └── api/              # mount of {{org}}/api
└── unify-error-format/   # task 2 — cross-repo change
    ├── api/              # mount of {{org}}/api
    └── web/              # mount of {{org}}/web
```

## Working on a task

- All edits go under `./<slug>/<repo>/`. Never write to the space root,
  to the task directory itself (only its repo subdirs are mounts), or to
  a sibling task's directory.
- `oak status`, `oak diff`, `oak log`, `oak commit`, `oak push` all
  work when run from inside a repo's mount
  (`cd ./<slug>/<repo> && oak status`). They route through that mount and
  operate on its virtual branch.
- **`oak commit` takes no message.** It accepts no `-m` flag — branch
  descriptions are the source of truth for "what happened," not
  per-commit messages. Just `oak commit`.
- The overlay (uncommitted changes) is the **active commit**. Run
  `oak commit` inside a mount to finalize a commit; the next edit starts
  a new active commit on top.
- Write the branch description to a temp file, then run `oak finish
  --desc-file <file>` from inside the repo mount. From the space root, use
  `oak mount finish ./<slug>/<repo> --desc-file <file>`. Both set the branch
  description, commit the active overlay if needed, push the virtual branch,
  and tear down the mount after the push succeeds. If finalization fails, it
  leaves the mount intact and names the next manual command to run.
- Each repo's mount is independent — commit, push, and describe them
  separately, even within one cross-repo task.

## At the end of every prompt — finalize automatically

When you finish responding to a prompt, **always** wrap up the task on
your own — no "should I commit?" check-in first. For **each repo you
touched**, run:

```
DESC_FILE="$(mktemp)"
cat > "$DESC_FILE" <<'DESC'
Short summary of the change.

Optional details about what changed and why.
DESC
cd ./<slug>/<repo>
oak finish --desc-file "$DESC_FILE"
cd ../..
```

This sets the squash-merge message from the file, commits the active overlay
if needed, pushes the virtual branch, and tears down the mount after the push
succeeds. Skip a repo only if `oak status` shows nothing changed there and it
does not need a new description.

Use `oak desc --file <file>` inside a mount only when you need to update the
description without finalizing yet. For normal prompt wrap-up, use
`oak finish --desc-file <file>` from inside the mount, or
`oak mount finish [path] --desc-file <file>` when you need to finish a mount
by path.

Report the branch name(s) and push result for each repo back to the user.

Run all of this every time, unattended. The branches are pushed and the
mounts are gone by the time you hand the turn back.

## Follow-ups — remount

If the user follows up after you've torn the mounts down, just bring up
fresh mounts and keep going:

```
oak mount {{org}}/<repo> ./<slug>/<repo>
```

Reuse the same `<slug>` (and repo subdir names) so the branches stay
recognizable, then pick up where you left off and finalize again at the
end with `oak finish --desc-file <file>` inside each repo mount as above.

A remount always branches off the repo's trunk — Oak's flat model
forbids mounting any other branch — so it starts from the latest trunk,
**not** from the branch you just pushed. If the follow-up needs to build
on that pushed work, merge it into the trunk first (or have the user
merge it), then remount.

To abandon a repo without pushing: `oak mount end --force ./<slug>/<repo>`
— `--force` discards any uncommitted overlay.

## Cleaning up

`oak space clean` tears down every mount in this space whose work is
fully saved — no uncommitted overlay and no unpushed commits — i.e.
tasks you've already committed and pushed/merged, across all their
repos. Mounts with in-flight work are left untouched so you don't lose
it; pass `--force` to discard and remove those too. Run it from the
space root, or `oak space clean <path>` to clean one task.

## Knowing what's in flight

- `oak mount list` shows every active mount on the machine with its
  virtual branch name. Run it when you're unsure what tasks/repos are
  mounted or before ending a session.
- `oak mount list --json` shows the same inventory in machine-readable form,
  including dirty overlay and unpushed commit counts.
- Inside a repo mount, `oak agent state --json --compact` prints the
  lowest-token preflight document with recommended next commands. Use full
  `oak agent state --json` when you need every field; add `--refresh` only
  when you explicitly want remote freshness fields and accept the network call.
- Use `oak status --porcelain`/`oak status --short`, `oak diff --name-only`,
  `oak diff --stat`, and `oak log --oneline -n N` before widening to full
  JSON or full diff output.
- `oak branch list --remote --json` lists remote branches without switching
  your current checkout or mount. Use `oak branch show|diff|review <branch>
  --remote --json` to triage a remote branch, and `oak close <branch>
  --remote --json` to close stale remote work.
- `oak space repos` re-lists the org's repos whenever you need to find
  another one to mount.

## Branch descriptions

`oak finish --desc-file <file>` reads a UTF-8 description file, writes it onto
the virtual branch, commits dirty overlay work if needed, pushes, and ends the
mount. `oak mount finish [path] --desc-file <file>` is the path-based form
for running the same finalization from outside the mount. `oak desc --file
<file>` also works inside a mount when you only need to update the
description; `oak desc --file -` reads from stdin when piping generated text
directly. The branch description becomes the squash-merge commit message when
the branch is merged into the repo's trunk, so it's worth writing a real
summary rather than relying on the slug fallback. For a cross-repo task, give
each repo's branch a description scoped to what changed in that repo.
